in @code
in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @nl
in @property

# @code/erlang.ast — the Erlang Abstract Format (EAF) substrate grammar.
#
# The first concrete `@code/<lang>.ast` shard. Opens the BEAM target
# road: future ticks wire `@code/erlang/macro` → `@code/erlang.ast`
# emission → `compile:forms/1` → `.beam` (the BEAM analogue of the
# existing Rust ouroboros pipeline at
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]] and
# `shards/spectral.mirror`'s step-by-step lift).
#
# === naming: `<lang>.ast`, not `<lang>/ast` ===
#
# The dotted form parallels the substrate's existing parametric AST
# vocabulary (`splinter(ast)` at `shards/glass.mirror`; `type ast(g)`
# at `boot/01-meta.mirror`). `<lang>.ast` reads as "the AST
# specialization of the `<lang>` grammar" — a type-altitude
# specialization, not a sub-path under a `<lang>` parent. The form
# is consistent with @code/metalogue's shim-return references
# (`shim_type(d: declaration) -> code/rust.ast` at
# `shards/code/metalogue.mirror` line 175 et al; `shim_*` in
# `shards/code/rust/macro.mirror` line 113-116). Path-namespace
# property per [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]: this
# file at `shards/code/erlang.ast.mirror` declares `@code/erlang.ast`.
# The dotted segment IS part of the name; the file name preserves it.
#
# === the substrate-pull recognition ===
#
# Per [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (52-instance
# track): `code/rust.ast` has been type-referenced from
# `@code/metalogue` and `@code/rust/macro` since 2026-06-09 without
# the type itself being declared in its own shard. The shim
# signatures consumed it as an abstract carrier and the four laws
# discharged via the per-species `requires` clauses. This tick
# lands the first explicit `@code/<lang>.ast` shard for Erlang
# because the BEAM-target road is opening now — `@code/rust.ast`
# will follow with the kintsugi-on-Rust cascade (T22+) when the
# Rust-side consumer pulls.
#
# === what this shard declares ===
#
# The EAF tuple-grammar lifted to substrate sum types. Per
# `erts/absform.html` (the canonical EAF spec), every Erlang
# abstract form is a tagged tuple `{Tag, ANNO, ...}` where ANNO is
# the annotation carrier (modern OTP: `erl_anno:anno()`; classic:
# a line integer). The substrate declarations below carry:
#
#   - `anno` — the annotation type. Modern superset: line + column
#     + extension attributes. The classic line-integer form is the
#     projection (the floor admits both; the substrate retains the
#     richer form so the carrier doesn't lose information at the
#     glass wall).
#   - `form` — the abstract_form() sum (top-level module forms).
#   - `expr` — the abstract_expr() sum (expressions).
#   - `pat` — the abstract_pat() sum (patterns; structurally
#     overlapping with `expr` per EAF but discriminated at the
#     parser altitude per `erl_parse(3)`'s pattern judgment).
#   - `clause` — the abstract_clause() record (the canonical
#     `{clause, ANNO, Patterns, Guards, Body}` shape).
#   - `type_form` — the abstract_type() sum (typespecs).
#   - `bin_element` — bitstring segment (used inside `bin`).
#   - `assoc` — map association (used inside `map`).
#   - `qualifier` — comprehension qualifier (used inside `lc` / `bc`
#     / `mc`).
#   - `guard` — a single conjunction of guard tests; the EAF guard
#     surface is `[[guard]]` (OR-of-ANDs).
#
# Coverage matches the EAF spec's full tag set per the erlang.org
# `erts/absform.html` reference. No "minimal floor" projection:
# EAF is a closed grammar specified by the BEAM team, and downstream
# codegen needs the full carrier so `compile:forms/1` can consume
# what the substrate emits. The substrate retains the closure; the
# species' emit step IS the projection back to the BEAM AST.
#
# === what this shard does NOT declare ===
#
# - The per-species shim bindings (`shim_erlang_function`,
#   `shim_erlang_module`, etc.). Per the @code/metalogue contract
#   (`shards/code/metalogue.mirror` §"the four shim signatures"),
#   shim bindings live at `@code/<lang>/macro`, NOT at
#   `@code/<lang>.ast`. The downstream `shards/code/erlang/macro.mirror`
#   tick will bind `shim_type` / `shim_prism` / `shim_action` /
#   `shim_grammar` to `code/erlang.ast` as the species AST type,
#   parallel to `shards/code/rust/macro.mirror` lines 113-116.
#
# - The `@code/erlang` altitude root. The brief's path layout
#   places `@code/erlang.ast` as a depth-1 sibling of `@code/rust`,
#   `@code/gleam`, `@code/mirror` — language is denoted by the bare
#   `<lang>` segment, `.ast` is the AST-specialization marker. A
#   future `shards/code/erlang.mirror` will declare the altitude
#   five-op prism block (parallel to `shards/code/rust.mirror`);
#   this tick lands the AST grammar alone because the BEAM road's
#   smallest tick is the carrier the macro shim will return.
#
# - The mirror.spec extension naming `target binary { altitude
#   @code/erlang, emit beam }`. That's Taut's territory
#   (`shards/mirror/{mosaic,spec,spec/keywords}.mirror`,
#   `mirror.spec`).
#
# - Distribution / port / NIF properties. Brief context names
#   them as downstream ticks (BEAM-native `:net_kernel`, ports
#   by default, `@epistemologic/property/nif_safe` + fracture
#   body for NIFs). The grammar floor doesn't carry the runtime
#   surface.
#
# === Spec context ===
#
# - erlang.org/doc/apps/erts/absform.html (the canonical EAF
#   spec; erts 16.0 / OTP 28).
# - docs/specs/code-metalogue-surface.md §4.1 (the four
#   substrate-declaration kinds the shim dispatches over;
#   `code/erlang.ast` is the species-AST carrier each `shim_X`
#   returns).
# - docs/specs/mirror-spec-schema.md §2 (target.emit), §10.5
#   (emit disambiguation) — the future `emit beam` keyword
#   will resolve through `@code/erlang`'s altitude when that
#   shard lands.
#
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]],
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]],
#                      [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]],
#                      [[architecture-splinter-ast-quote-primitive]]
#                      (the parametric AST-fragment construction
#                      primitive; `splinter(@code/erlang.ast)` is
#                      the natural composition at the BEAM altitude).
#
# Related shards: code.mirror (universal parent),
#                 code/rust.mirror (the sibling Rust altitude root;
#                  precedent for `@code/<lang>` family member),
#                 code/gleam.mirror (sibling altitude root; same
#                  family pattern),
#                 code/metalogue.mirror (the universal AST-altitude
#                  metalogue ground; declares `shim_*(d) ->
#                  species_ast` parametric in the species AST type),
#                 code/rust/macro.mirror (the Rust-altitude
#                  realisation; binds shims to `code/rust.ast`;
#                  the parallel future for Erlang lives at
#                  `shards/code/erlang/macro.mirror`),
#                 glass.mirror (declares `splinter(ast)`; the
#                  AST-specialization mechanism),
#                 spectral.mirror (the runtime family; future
#                  BEAM runtime targets land here).
#
# Per [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]: `prism
# @code/erlang.ast { ... }` declares the family-root-at-depth-1
# parallel to `@code/rust`'s `prism` block. Depth-≥2 sub-prisms
# (e.g. `@code/erlang.ast/<subtree>`) would use `glass` per
# recognition #46; the depth-0/1 boundary uses `prism`.

prism @code/erlang.ast {
  focus erlang_ast
  project erlang_ast
  split erlang_ast
  shift erlang_ast
  settle erlang_ast
}

# === annotation type (ANNO) ===
#
# EAF's `ANNO` is `erl_anno:anno()` in modern OTP. Carries line +
# column + an extensible attribute list (file ref, generated flag,
# record-line context, etc.). The classic line-integer form is the
# floor projection.
#
# The substrate retains the richer form because the carrier is
# what `compile:forms/1` will consume on the wire — collapsing to a
# bare line integer at the substrate altitude would force every
# downstream emit step to reconstruct what the parser already had.
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]]: `anno` is a newtype around the
# substrate fields, not a bare integer.
#
# `anno_attr` is the extension key/value cell. EAF's wire form
# carries: `{file, ref}`, `{generated, bool}`, `{record, ref}`,
# plus implementation-extensible variants. The substrate models
# the closed core variants explicitly and admits unknown extensions
# via `attr_other(ref, ref)` so the carrier is total under
# round-trip.
type anno = {
  line: u32,
  column: option(u32),
  attrs: [anno_attr],
}

type anno_attr =
  | attr_file(ref)
  | attr_generated(bool)
  | attr_record(ref)
  | attr_text(ref)
  | attr_other(ref, ref)

# === abstract_form() — top-level module forms ===
#
# Per `erts/absform.html` §"Module declarations and forms": every
# top-level Erlang form is one of: module attribute, export, import,
# file, function definition, spec / callback attribute, record
# declaration, type / opaque declaration, end-of-file marker, plus
# wild attributes.
#
# The substrate enumeration matches the EAF tag set 1-to-1; each
# variant carries its `anno` plus the per-tag payload from the EAF
# spec. The wild-attribute variant (`form_attribute_wild`) catches
# user-defined attributes per EAF's catch-all `{attribute, ANNO,
# A, T}`.
type form =
  | form_module(anno, ref)
  | form_export(anno, [func_ref])
  | form_import(anno, ref, [func_ref])
  | form_file(anno, ref, u32)
  | form_function(anno, ref, u32, [clause])
  | form_spec(anno, func_ref, [func_type])
  | form_remote_spec(anno, ref, func_ref, [func_type])
  | form_callback(anno, func_ref, [func_type])
  | form_record(anno, ref, [record_field_decl])
  | form_type(anno, ref, type_form, [type_var])
  | form_opaque(anno, ref, type_form, [type_var])
  | form_attribute_wild(anno, ref, ref)
  | form_eof(anno)

# `func_ref` — the `{Name, Arity}` cell used in exports / imports
# / specs. `mod_func_ref` is the remote form `{Module, Name,
# Arity}`. Declared as records (not bare tuples) so the type system
# names what each field IS per [[feedback-no-bare-types]].
type func_ref = {
  name: ref,
  arity: u32,
}

type mod_func_ref = {
  module: ref,
  name: ref,
  arity: u32,
}

# === abstract_expr() — expressions ===
#
# Per `erts/absform.html` §"Expressions": every Erlang expression
# is one of the variants below. The literal expressions (`atom`,
# `char`, `float`, `integer`, `string`, `nil`, `var`) recur in the
# pattern grammar (declared separately below); structurally they
# share the same `(anno, payload)` shape but the discrimination
# context (expression vs pattern) is part of the form's well-
# typedness per `erl_parse(3)`'s pattern judgment.
#
# Recursive references: expressions can contain other expressions
# (`call`, `cons`, `match`, `case`, `tuple`, `block`, `bin`, `lc`,
# `bc`, `mc`, `map`, `if`, `receive`, `try`, `fun`, etc.) and can
# contain clauses (`case`, `if`, `receive`, `try`, `fun`,
# `named_fun`). The mutual recursion expr ↔ clause ↔ pattern is
# the EAF's structural shape; substrate carries it directly.
type expr =
  | expr_atom(anno, ref)
  | expr_char(anno, u32)
  | expr_float(anno, f64)
  | expr_integer(anno, i64)
  | expr_string(anno, ref)
  | expr_nil(anno)
  | expr_var(anno, ref)
  | expr_bin(anno, [bin_element])
  | expr_bc(anno, expr, [qualifier])
  | expr_block(anno, [expr])
  | expr_case(anno, expr, [clause])
  | expr_catch(anno, expr)
  | expr_cons(anno, expr, expr)
  | expr_fun_ref(anno, func_ref)
  | expr_fun_remote_ref(anno, mod_func_ref)
  | expr_fun_clauses(anno, [clause])
  | expr_named_fun(anno, ref, [clause])
  | expr_call(anno, expr, [expr])
  | expr_remote_call(anno, expr, expr, [expr])
  | expr_if(anno, [clause])
  | expr_lc(anno, expr, [qualifier])
  | expr_mc(anno, assoc, [qualifier])
  | expr_map(anno, [assoc])
  | expr_map_update(anno, expr, [assoc])
  | expr_match(anno, pat, expr)
  | expr_maybe_match(anno, pat, expr)
  | expr_maybe(anno, [expr])
  | expr_maybe_else(anno, [expr], [clause])
  | expr_op_unary(anno, ref, expr)
  | expr_op_binary(anno, ref, expr, expr)
  | expr_receive(anno, [clause])
  | expr_receive_after(anno, [clause], expr, [expr])
  | expr_record(anno, ref, [record_field_set])
  | expr_record_update(anno, expr, ref, [record_field_set])
  | expr_record_field(anno, expr, ref, ref)
  | expr_record_index(anno, ref, ref)
  | expr_tuple(anno, [expr])
  | expr_try(anno, [expr], [clause], [clause], [expr])

# === abstract_pat() — patterns ===
#
# Per `erts/absform.html` §"Patterns": the pattern grammar is a
# proper subset of the expression grammar (no calls, no `case`,
# no `if`, no comprehensions, no `try`, etc.). Discriminated
# separately because the parser's pattern judgment is what
# enforces the subset — substrate carries the subset as its own
# sum type so type-soundness at the shim altitude is structural,
# not a runtime check.
#
# The `pat_var` variant covers `'_'` (the wildcard pattern) per
# EAF's "var with name `'_'`" convention; no separate wildcard
# constructor.
type pat =
  | pat_atom(anno, ref)
  | pat_char(anno, u32)
  | pat_float(anno, f64)
  | pat_integer(anno, i64)
  | pat_string(anno, ref)
  | pat_nil(anno)
  | pat_var(anno, ref)
  | pat_bin(anno, [bin_element])
  | pat_match(anno, pat, pat)
  | pat_cons(anno, pat, pat)
  | pat_map(anno, [assoc])
  | pat_op_unary(anno, ref, pat)
  | pat_op_binary(anno, ref, pat, pat)
  | pat_record_index(anno, ref, ref)
  | pat_record(anno, ref, [record_field_set])
  | pat_tuple(anno, [pat])

# === abstract_clause() — clauses ===
#
# Per `erts/absform.html` §"Clauses": every Erlang clause is
# `{clause, ANNO, Patterns, Guards, Body}`. Guards nest as
# OR-of-ANDs: `[[guard_test]]` — the outer list is disjunction,
# each inner `guard` is a conjunction of `guard_test`s. The
# substrate names the structure with typed records, not nested
# bare lists.
#
# `guard_test` is structurally a subset of `expr` (only certain
# BIFs and operations are guard-safe per `erl_lint`). At the
# substrate floor we carry `expr` and admit the subset as the
# guard-safety property declared at the @property altitude (a
# future tick: `@epistemologic/property/guard_safe(expr)`).
type clause = {
  anno: anno,
  patterns: [pat],
  guards: [guard],
  body: [expr],
}

type guard = {
  tests: [expr],
}

# === supporting carriers ===

# `bin_element` — bitstring segment used inside `expr_bin` and
# `pat_bin`. Per EAF: `{bin_element, ANNO, Value, Size,
# TypeSpecList}` where Size is `default` or an expression; TSL is
# a list of type specifiers (or `default`).
type bin_element = {
  anno: anno,
  value: expr,
  size: bin_size,
  type_specs: bin_type_specs,
}

type bin_size =
  | size_default
  | size_expr(expr)

type bin_type_specs =
  | tsl_default
  | tsl_specs([bin_type_spec])

type bin_type_spec =
  | bts_atom(ref)
  | bts_pair(ref, u32)

# `assoc` — map association used inside `expr_map`, `expr_map_update`,
# `pat_map`, `expr_mc`. Per EAF: `{map_field_assoc, ANNO, K, V}`
# for `K => V` and `{map_field_exact, ANNO, K, V}` for `K := V`.
type assoc =
  | assoc_field(anno, expr, expr)
  | assoc_exact(anno, expr, expr)

# `qualifier` — comprehension qualifier used inside `expr_lc`,
# `expr_bc`, `expr_mc`. Per EAF: `{generate, ANNO, P, E}` (list
# gen), `{b_generate, ANNO, P, E}` (bitstring gen),
# `{m_generate, ANNO, A, E}` (map gen), or a filter expression.
# The filter form is a bare expression at the EAF tuple altitude
# (no wrapping tag) but the substrate names it explicitly so the
# sum is total.
type qualifier =
  | gen_list(anno, pat, expr)
  | gen_bin(anno, pat, expr)
  | gen_map(anno, assoc, expr)
  | gen_filter(expr)

# `record_field_decl` — field declaration inside `form_record`. Per
# EAF: `{record_field, ANNO, {atom, ANNO, Name}}` or `{record_field,
# ANNO, {atom, ANNO, Name}, DefaultExpr}` or
# `{typed_record_field, RecordField, Type}`.
type record_field_decl = {
  anno: anno,
  name: ref,
  default: option(expr),
  field_type: option(type_form),
}

# `record_field_set` — field set in record-creation and
# record-update expressions. Per EAF: `{record_field, ANNO,
# {atom, ANNO, Name}, V}` with `V` an expression in expression
# context or a pattern in pattern context. The substrate carries
# `expr` here; the discrimination follows from the enclosing form
# (`expr_record` vs `pat_record`).
type record_field_set = {
  anno: anno,
  name: ref,
  value: expr,
}

# === abstract_type() — typespecs ===
#
# Per `erts/absform.html` §"Types": EAF carries typespec forms as
# `{type, ANNO, Tag, Args}`, `{remote_type, ANNO, [Mod, Name,
# Args]}`, `{user_type, ANNO, Name, Args}`, `{ann_type, ANNO,
# [Var, Type]}`, `{var, ANNO, Name}`, plus the literal forms
# (`atom`, `integer`, `char`).
#
# The substrate enumerates the closed type-tag set (binary, nil,
# 'fun', range, map, tuple, union, record, product, bounded_fun,
# constraint, map_field_assoc, map_field_exact, field_type) as
# explicit variants. The predefined / built-in tag form
# `{type, ANNO, N, [TypeArgs]}` where `N` is a built-in name
# (integer, atom, list, ...) is carried as `type_predefined(anno,
# ref, [type_form])` so the carrier is total.
#
# `func_type` is the bounded function type used in `form_spec` /
# `form_callback`; declared separately because spec / callback
# carry a list of function types (the form's overload set).
type type_form =
  | type_ann(anno, ref, type_form)
  | type_atom(anno, ref)
  | type_char(anno, u32)
  | type_integer(anno, i64)
  | type_binary(anno, type_form, type_form)
  | type_nil(anno)
  | type_fun_any(anno)
  | type_fun_return(anno, type_form)
  | type_range(anno, type_form, type_form)
  | type_map_any(anno)
  | type_map(anno, [type_form])
  | type_tuple_any(anno)
  | type_tuple(anno, [type_form])
  | type_union(anno, [type_form])
  | type_record(anno, ref, [type_form])
  | type_product(anno, [type_form])
  | type_predefined(anno, ref, [type_form])
  | type_map_field_assoc(anno, type_form, type_form)
  | type_map_field_exact(anno, type_form, type_form)
  | type_field(anno, ref, type_form)
  | type_remote(anno, ref, ref, [type_form])
  | type_user(anno, ref, [type_form])
  | type_var(anno, ref)
  | type_op_unary(anno, ref, type_form)
  | type_op_binary(anno, ref, type_form, type_form)

type func_type = {
  anno: anno,
  params: [type_form],
  return_type: type_form,
  constraints: [type_constraint],
}

type type_constraint = {
  anno: anno,
  var: ref,
  bound: type_form,
}

type type_var = {
  anno: anno,
  name: ref,
}

# === splinter(@code/erlang.ast) — the addressable AST fragment ===
#
# Per [[architecture-splinter-ast-quote-primitive]] (#54): the
# substrate's parametric quote primitive specializes by species.
# `splinter(@code/erlang.ast)` is the content-addressed,
# oid-identified EAF fragment future fracture bodies emit when
# discharging a `\` obligation at the Erlang altitude. The
# declaration here names the specialization; the carrier shape
# inherits from `splinter(ast)` (shards/glass.mirror line 261).
#
# Body abstract per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]: a consumer
# pull from `@code/erlang/macro` (the downstream tick) will
# discharge this when its `shim_*` realisations need to emit
# typed AST fragments declaratively rather than reconstruct
# tuples at the call site.

type erlang_ast_splinter = splinter(@code/erlang.ast)

# === out ===

out @code/erlang.ast
out anno
out anno_attr
out form
out func_ref
out mod_func_ref
out expr
out pat
out clause
out guard
out bin_element
out bin_size
out bin_type_specs
out bin_type_spec
out assoc
out qualifier
out record_field_decl
out record_field_set
out type_form
out func_type
out type_constraint
out type_var
out erlang_ast_splinter
